From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 20:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] accel/rocket: complete jobs that never reach the hardware

rocket_job_run() takes a runtime-PM reference and then attaches the IOMMU
group.  Both of its early returns leave the job half-started: the fence
handed back to the scheduler is one nothing will ever signal, and the
runtime-PM reference is one nothing will ever drop.  core->in_flight_job
is still NULL at that point, and both paths that would balance the
reference -- the completion in rocket_job_handle_irq() and the timeout in
rocket_reset() -- only put when a job is in flight.

Two separate things then pin the NPU runtime-active, and each is enough on
its own:

  - The usage counter ratchets.  pm_runtime_get_sync() takes its reference
    before it tries to resume and keeps it when the resume fails, so the
    caller owes a put; the IOMMU path has already resumed successfully and
    owes an ordinary put.  Neither happens.  The counter never returns to
    zero, so the device can never runtime-suspend again.

  - The scheduler never gets its credit back.  The unsignalled fence keeps
    the job pending forever, and rocket_device_runtime_suspend() returns
    -EBUSY for as long as a credit is outstanding.  This one holds the
    device active even with the usage counter balanced.

The usage-counter half outlives the driver.  The reference is on the
platform device, which the OF core owns, so rmmod/insmod does not clear
it: the freshly probed device comes back active and stays there until a
reboot.

Measured on an H96 MAX M9 (RK3576) by failing the attach on one job.
Before, a single failure became a self-sustaining storm -- the job timed
out at 500 ms, rocket_reset() called rocket_core_reset(), which takes the
IOMMU down ("MMU_DTE_ADDR is not functioning"), so the next attach failed
for real.  One injected failure produced seven, and leaked seven
references; the device then stayed active across a module reload.  After,
the injected failure is the only one: the job is retired with its error,
the caller's next submit attaches and computes normally, no timeout fires,
and the device suspends.  RK3576 gates are unchanged (conv 102/102, conv
library 111 + 1 refused, matmul 43/43, refusal 9/9).

This is not RK3576-specific -- the error paths are the same on RK3588, and
that part reaches them through the same rocket_core_reset().  It is in
this series only because this is where a part that wedges often enough to
notice lives.

Signed-off-by: gregordinary <33880035+gregordinary@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
+++ b/drivers/accel/rocket/rocket_job.c
@@ -372,12 +372,26 @@
 	job->done_fence = dma_fence_get(fence);
 
 	ret = pm_runtime_get_sync(core->dev);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return fence;
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		/*
+		 * pm_runtime_get_sync() takes the usage reference before it
+		 * tries to resume and keeps it when the resume fails, so the
+		 * caller owes the put.
+		 */
+		pm_runtime_put_noidle(core->dev);
+		goto err_unstarted;
+	}
 
 	ret = iommu_attach_group(job->domain->domain, core->iommu_group);
-	if (ret < 0)
-		return fence;
+	if (ret < 0) {
+		/*
+		 * core->in_flight_job is still NULL, so neither the completion
+		 * path nor the timeout path will drop this reference.
+		 */
+		pm_runtime_mark_last_busy(core->dev);
+		pm_runtime_put_autosuspend(core->dev);
+		goto err_unstarted;
+	}
 
 	scoped_guard(mutex, &core->job_lock) {
 		core->in_flight_job = job;
@@ -385,6 +399,19 @@
 	}
 
 	return fence;
+
+err_unstarted:
+	/*
+	 * The job never reached the hardware, so nothing will ever signal this
+	 * fence.  Complete it with the error, or the scheduler keeps the job
+	 * pending and never takes its credit back -- and
+	 * rocket_device_runtime_suspend() refuses for as long as a credit is
+	 * outstanding, which pins the NPU runtime-active for good.
+	 */
+	dma_fence_set_error(fence, ret);
+	dma_fence_signal(fence);
+
+	return fence;
 }
 
 static void rocket_job_handle_irq(struct rocket_core *core);
